JsEdit - a programmer's editor
==============================

JsEdit is a text editor for programs, in the style of the programmers'
editors of the time (StrongED, Zap). It colours JavaScript, BBC BASIC,
Obey and JSON as you type, numbers the lines, keeps your indentation, can
complete names, and runs JavaScript programs - showing you any errors on
the lines they happened. It works like Edit, so everything you know from
Edit (the menus, Find, the keys) works here too.

Starting
--------
Double-click !JsEdit. Its icon appears on the icon bar.

  Select on the icon     a new JavaScript program
  Menu on the icon       Info, Create (a new file of another kind),
                         Throwback, Quit
  Drag a file onto it    edit that file

Shift-double-click a JSScript file in a directory display to edit it in
JsEdit. (A plain double-click runs the program, as always.)

The toolbar
-----------
Every window has a toolbar across the top:

  Save        save the text (F3)
  Run         save the program and run it (Ctrl-R)
  Check       check the program's syntax
  Find, Goto  as in Edit (F4, F5)
  Functions   a list of the functions in the text: click one to go to it
  Errors      the Throwback window
  Mode        on the right: the text's mode and where the caret is;
              click it to change the mode

Programs
--------
While you type, JsEdit checks the syntax of a JavaScript program and marks
the first mistake in the margin, with the line shaded. Run saves the
program, checks it and runs it (an application's !RunImage runs the whole
application). If the program goes wrong while it runs, the error comes
back to JsEdit ("throwback"): the Throwback window lists it, the line is
marked, and the caret goes to it. Click an error in the Throwback window
to go to it again.

Keys
----
Edit's keys work as in Edit, and also:

  Return          new line, indented like the one before (and one step
                  more after an opening bracket)
  Tab             indent (the selected lines, or to the next step)
  Shift-Tab       outdent
  Ctrl-Space      complete the name before the caret
  Ctrl-R          run the program
  Up, Down,       choose from the completion list; Return or Tab puts
  Return, Escape  the name in, Escape closes the list

Completion
----------
As you type a name, JsEdit offers names that could finish it: the
desktop's programming interface (print, task.every, w.useCanvas ...), with
a line about each, JavaScript's own words, and names already in the text.
Display > Completion turns this off; Ctrl-Space still asks for it.

Modes
-----
How each kind of file is coloured, indented and completed is set by a
mode: a text file in !JsEdit.Modes (Shift-double-click !JsEdit to open
it). Change them with JsEdit (or Edit) and restart JsEdit. Each line is
one setting:

  Name       JavaScript
  Lexer      c-like            (c-like, basic, obey or text)
  Types      &F81              file types that use the mode
  Indent     2                 spaces per step
  Comment    //                comment to the end of the line
  Block      /* */             block comments
  Quotes     ' " `             string quotes
  Keywords   if else ...       keywords (lines add up)
  Constants  true false ...
  Api        task print ...
  Functions  <pattern>         finds definitions for Functions (a regular
                               expression; the name is in brackets)
  Colour     keyword 8 bold    a Wimp colour (0-15) or #rrggbb for a kind
                               of text: text comment string number
                               keyword constant api regex punct lineno
                               command variable
  Dark       keyword #569cd6   the same, for the dark theme
  Complete   name(args) about  an entry for the completion list

Display > Line numbers, Bold keywords, Caret line, Completion and Dark
theme switch those on and off; the rest of the Display menu is Edit's.
Dark theme shows the text in light colours on a dark background; a mode's
Dark lines give its colours for it (Dark keyword #569cd6 bold).

Fonts
-----
JsEdit uses the system font unless you choose another from Display > Font
list. Three fonts made for programming are there as well as the RISC OS
fonts: JetBrainsMono, Hack and FiraCode (Medium and Bold). They are "Nerd
Fonts", which also hold thousands of icons that programs can draw
(g.fillText('\uf121', x, y) in the font "JetBrainsMono Nerd").

JsEdit is new: RISC OS 3.71 came with Edit only. The book "Programming in
JavaScript" (Manuals.JSTutor) uses it.
